After creating a GitHub account, prioritize security and identity configuration to ensure proper commit attribution and account protection. Verify your email address immediately, as this is required to link commits to your profile and access all features.  Enable two-factor authentication (2FA) using an authenticator app or passkey, as this is strongly recommended for account safety and may be required for certain organizational access.

In Settings, configure the following critical options:

Email Privacy: Under Settings > Emails, check Keep my email addresses private and Block command line pushes that expose my email to prevent your personal address from appearing in public commit history. 
Authentication: Set up secure access by generating an SSH key (under Settings > SSH and GPG keys) or a Personal Access Token (under Developer settings) to use instead of a password for Git operations. 
Profile Personalization: Edit your Public profile to add a professional username, bio, profile picture, and location; consider creating a profile README by making a repository named exactly after your username.
Local Git Configuration: On your local machine, run git config --global user.name "Your Name" and git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com" to match the verified email on your GitHub account, ensuring your contributions are tracked correctly.

