Advanced GitHub setup tips
Here's what else is worth doing beyond the basics:

Security & Recovery

Store your 2FA recovery codes in a password manager immediately after enabling 2FA — without them, a lost phone locks you out. 
Add a backup email (Settings → Emails) so you can recover the account if your primary address becomes inaccessible.
Add a passkey (Settings → Password and authentication) for passwordless, biometric login on trusted devices.
Use a long-term email (personal, not a work address) so you don't lose account access when you change jobs. 
Local Git Hardening

Use the GitHub noreply email (ID+username@users.noreply.github.com) as your git config --global user.email — it's the anonymized address GitHub generates when you enable email privacy. 
Consider commit signing with SSH keys (git config --global commit.gpgsign true + git config --global gpg.format ssh) so your commits are cryptographically verified.
Profile & Visibility

Pin 4–6 quality repos to your profile (Settings → Public profile → Pinned repositories) — curated projects beat a wall of forks. 
Add topics/tags to each repo (Settings on the repo → About → Topics) for discoverability.
Add a website/LinkedIn link to your profile sidebar.
If you have private work you want to show activity for, enable "Include private contributions on my profile" (Settings → Public profile) — it shows green squares without exposing commits. 
Check for shadow-ban: open your profile URL in an incognito window. If you see a 404, your new account may be flagged by GitHub's spam filters. 
Ongoing Habits

Write meaningful commit messages (not "fix" or "update") — your history is a portfolio.
Aim for consistent small contributions rather than sporadic large dumps.
Make at least one open-source contribution early (even a docs fix) to build your reputation.
